  <span class="hero-ornament"></span>
  <h1 class="research-title">History Now</h1>
  <p class="ref-lede">What is being found and written about the past this week, the round anniversaries of the next thirty days, and what is new at the Institute.</p>
</header>

<section class="now-sec now-sec-news" id="news" aria-labelledby="now-news-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-news-t">In the news</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Headlines from other publications, newest first, each with the Institute&rsquo;s page on its subject. The headlines, the articles and the thumbnails their feeds offer belong to their publishers: the Institute has not written or checked them. A headline without a publisher&rsquo;s thumbnail shows the picture of the Institute&rsquo;s page instead. The list is refreshed twice a day.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="news" data-max="10"><li class="now-wait">The headlines need JavaScript.</li></ol>
  <p class="now-note now-src">From the news pages of <a href="https://archaeology.org/news/" rel="noopener">Archaeology Magazine</a>, <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/" rel="noopener">Smithsonian Magazine</a>, <a href="https://phys.org/science-news/archaeology/" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a>, <a href="https://archaeologymag.com/" rel="noopener">Archaeology News</a>, <a href="https://www.medievalists.net/" rel="noopener">Medievalists.net</a>, <a href="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/" rel="noopener">The History Blog</a>. A headline is listed only when it names a page of the Institute.</p>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="anniversaries" aria-labelledby="now-ann-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-ann-t">Coming anniversaries</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Round anniversaries in the next thirty days: centuries, half-centuries, quarter-centuries of the last three hundred years and the decades of the last hundred. Every day has its own page in <a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a>.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="ann" data-max="40"><li class="now-wait">The anniversaries need JavaScript.</li></ol>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="new" aria-labelledby="now-new-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-new-t">New at the Institute</h2>
  <ol class="wn-list">
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-22">22 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Faiths Side by Side</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page sets the thirty religions beside one another: where and when each began, who founded or first taught it, its scriptures and its followers today. Each row links the lives of the founders, the sacred texts and a map of the faith’s spread; on a phone each faith is a card that unfolds.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/religions">Faiths Side by Side</a> <a href="/faiths">Faith Maps</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-22">22 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Sacred texts among the Great Works</h3><p class="wn-s">Twelve new profiles: the Hebrew Bible, the New Testament, the Quran, the Rigveda, the Upanishads, the Bhagavad Gita, the Pali Canon, the Lotus Sutra, the Avesta, the Talmud, the Guru Granth Sahib and the I Ching. Each tells how the text came about, what it holds, how it was handed down and how it has been read, giving what its tradition holds and what scholars conclude side by side.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=work">Great Works</a> <a href="/work/quran">Quran</a> <a href="/work/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> <a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-22">22 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Founders, prophets and saints among the Great Lives</h3><p class="wn-s">Twelve new lives: Jesus, Muhammad, the Buddha, Moses, Zoroaster, Guru Nanak, Laozi, Mahavira, Paul the Apostle, Ali, Abraham and Mary. Each is told as its tradition holds and as historians assess, with what is disputed said plainly, and eighty new encyclopedia entries cover the people, places and teachings around them, from the Sermon on the Mount and the Night Journey to the Four Noble Truths and the Gathas.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=life">Great Lives</a> <a href="/life/jesus">Jesus</a> <a href="/life/muhammad">Muhammad</a> <a href="/life/the-buddha">The Buddha</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Today's states on the map</h3><p class="wn-s">The atlas, the states register and the country histories now reach the present day for every country: 77 states were added, among them Russia, Japan, Poland, Ukraine, both Koreas, Nigeria and most of Africa and the Pacific, with partly recognised states such as Kosovo and the Sahrawi Republic described as such. Sixty countries that had no encyclopedia entry, from Kenya and Colombia to Kazakhstan and Tuvalu, now have one.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/atlas?year=2025">The atlas today</a> <a href="/state/russia">Russia</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">A calmer home page</h3><p class="wn-s">The home page now opens with four ways in (look around, follow a story, play, look something up), a row for researchers and teachers, twelve pictures to look through, the day in history and the day's games. Everything else is a click away in the menus and on Explore Everything.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/">Home</a> <a href="/research">Explore Everything</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Looks with a character of their own</h3><p class="wn-s">Illuminated opens each section with a gold or red-ink initial, Marble has faint veins and bronze rules, Winter a cold light and a frosted header, and High contrast underlines every link with a thicker focus ring. The look picker shows each one's colours.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">An entry in Illuminated</a></p></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="now-note"><a href="/new">Everything that is new, and every page added in the last thirty days &rarr;</a></p>
